}

steps.body:
Most grid questions are sums along a line. For row totals, the outer loop goes over rows: start a total at 0 for each row, and let the inner loop add that row's cells. For column totals, swap the loops so the outer loop goes over columns and the inner loop walks down the rows.

```cpp
for (std::size_t c = 0; c < grid[0].size(); ++c) {
    int total = 0;
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < grid.size(); ++r) {
        total += grid[r][c];
    }
    std::cout << ' ' << total;
}
```

Using `grid[0].size()` as the column count assumes a rectangular grid where every row has the same length.

Diagonals only exist on a square n by n grid, and each needs just one loop. The main diagonal runs top-left to bottom-right, where the row equals the column: `grid[i][i]`. The anti-diagonal runs top-right to bottom-left: `grid[i][n - 1 - i]`. Because `i` stays below `n`, `n - 1 - i` never drops below 0, so it is safe with `std::size_t`.

Row-by-row loops read each row's buffer in order, which is the cache-friendly pattern. Column loops jump between rows, which is fine for small grids.

example.code:
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    const std::vector<std::vector<int>> grid = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}, {7, 8, 9}};

    std::cout << "Row sums:";
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < grid.size(); ++r) {
        int total = 0;
        for (std::size_t c = 0; c < grid[r].size(); ++c) {
            total += grid[r][c];
        }
        std::cout << ' ' << total;
    }
    std::cout << '\n';

    std::cout << "Column sums:";
    for (std::size_t c = 0; c < grid[0].size(); ++c) {
        int total = 0;
        for (std::size_t r = 0; r < grid.size(); ++r) {
            total += grid[r][c];
        }
        std::cout << ' ' << total;
    }
    std::cout << '\n';

    const std::size_t n = grid.size();
    int mainSum = 0;
    int antiSum = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        mainSum += grid[i][i];
        antiSum += grid[i][n - 1 - i];
    }
    std::cout << "Diagonals: " << mainSum << ' ' << antiSum << '\n';
    return 0;
}

